 to hand down a positive  legacy  nate callaway is the great great  grandson  of hotelier jb stratford he had already  started several businesses  put himself through college put himself  through law school but his crowning  achievement was  his hotel which was the largest black  owned hotel  in america all three are a striking  representation of greenwood during that  time  greenwood was a place where people could  come with their vision  of a better world something  anthropologist marlon hall  is passionate about preserving if you  were here  you would see the hustle and the bustle  of a metropolis  you would see this ecologically friendly  community that had  every kind of store you imagined the  folkloric motto of this community was  if we didn't have it you don't need it  black families flocked to the oklahoma  territory fleeing a post-civil war  south these black people went into a  space that they thought they could  actually fulfill their dreams of what it  means to be free  manifest their freedom and they did but  amidst all that success  there were growing tensions resentment  by the white community  scapegoating blaming the black community  greenwood  for the vice in the community by 1907  oklahoma became a state quickly enacting  segregation and voter suppression laws  like  others in the south that helped give  rise to  even more division it was a powder keg  looking for a match so to speak  on may 31 1921 an  elevator ride in a downtown office  building  lit that keg a young man named dick  rowland was accused of  trying to rape a young white woman named  sarah page  someone heard a scream and the  assumption was that he was the attacker  my great grandfather was very attuned to  what was happening  he made the call to the local black men  that we have to go down to the  courthouse and stop  this mob so as the mob grew tensions  grew  things got out of hand a shot was fired  by dawn  several violent clashes had broken out  the most destructive  by air 